  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Pick, Store, Buy: Shelf life extension and reduced waste for tomato production in Nigeria</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Nigeria is the 14th largest tomato producer in the world and the largest in West Africa, with 48 million, predominantly smallholder growers (&lt;0.15 Ha plot size) cultivating 1.8 million tonnes of tomatoes every year.  However, 50% or 0.9 million tonnes of tomatoes rot and never reach consumers or processors; this situation is exacerbated by population demand of 2.5 million tonnes and leaves Nigeria with an annual supply deficit of 1.6 million tonnes.  Import substitution, supply chain losses and price hikes because of poor availability, currently costs Nigeria, $15 billion per year.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project will develop a shelf life extension and prediction tool to support critical business decisions and improve the accurate flow of fresh produce to the customer, increase revenue to the supply base, to alleviate poverty and also create new higher skilled jobs for the agri-food sector. Pick, Store, Buy overcomes limitations in the state-of-the-art and delivers the following technical innovations: i) A unique shelf life extension system that senses and conditions the biological status of fresh produce ii) Eradication of waste in the fresh produce supply chain and iii) The development of a novel business model which has knowledge exchange at its core to ease the transition to accessible innovative affordable technology which will transform the livelihoods of primary producers.</narrative>
  </description>
